{"evidence":[{"sentence_id":0,"text":"H. Sawin Millett Jr. (born October 8, 1937) is a Maine politician.","title":"Sawin Millett"},{"sentence_id":1,"text":" A Republican, Millett served as the Commissioner of Administrative and Financial Services in the administration of Governor Paul LePage from 2011 to 2014.","title":"Sawin Millett"},{"sentence_id":2,"text":" In January 2011, Millett was unanimously approved as Commissioner by the Maine Senate, with key Democrats also endorsing his appointment.","title":"Sawin Millett"},{"sentence_id":3,"text":" In December 2011, a poll of policymakers by the Bangor Daily News ranked Millett as the ninth most influential person in Maine politics.","title":"Sawin Millett"},{"sentence_id":0,"text":"The Bangor Daily News is an American newspaper covering a large portion of rural Maine, published six days per week in Bangor, Maine.","title":"Bangor Daily News"},{"sentence_id":0,"text":"Lawrence E. Lockman is an American lumber worker, lobbyist and political activist from Amherst, Maine, who has served as a Republican member of the Maine House of Representatives since 2012.","title":"Lawrence Lockman"},{"sentence_id":1,"text":" A 2014 article in "Bangor Daily News" documenting controversial statements he has made over the years on the subjects of rape, HIV, and homosexuality, has drawn attention from political websites such as "Daily Kos" and "Salon.com".","title":"Lawrence Lockman"},{"sentence_id":0,"text":"Ralph W. 'Bud' Leavitt Jr. (1917–1994) was a Maine newspaperman who was executive sports editor of "The Bangor Daily News", and a longtime outdoor columnist recognized statewide.","title":"Ralph W. 'Bud' Leavitt Jr."},{"sentence_id":1,"text":" In addition to his writing, Leavitt hosted one of Maine's earliest television shows, which was devoted to fishing, hunting and the out-of-doors.","title":"Ralph W. 'Bud' Leavitt Jr."},{"sentence_id":2,"text":" Leavitt's stature within the state was such that columnist Roy Blount Jr. wrote of the Maine sportswriter that he "fishes with Ted Williams and Red Smith – or, rather, they trout-fish with him."","title":"Ralph W. 'Bud' Leavitt Jr."},{"sentence_id":3,"text":" An early conservationist and son of a paper mill union leader, Leavitt urged the state of Maine to acquire lands for public use; one such preserve is today named for the sportswriter and television personality.","title":"Ralph W. 'Bud' Leavitt Jr."},{"sentence_id":0,"text":"Mildred Brown "Brownie" Schrumpf (January 24, 1903 – March 2, 2001) was an American home economist, food educator, and author.","title":"Mildred Brown Schrumpf"},{"sentence_id":1,"text":" Named the "Unofficial Ambassador of Good Eating" by the Maine Department of Agriculture, she wrote a weekly food column for the "Bangor Daily News" from 1951 to 1994 promoting traditional Maine recipes.","title":"Mildred Brown Schrumpf"},{"sentence_id":2,"text":" She was the main proponent of the claim that the chocolate brownie was invented in Bangor.","title":"Mildred Brown Schrumpf"},{"sentence_id":3,"text":" She was inducted into the Maine Women's Hall of Fame in 1997.","title":"Mildred Brown Schrumpf"},{"sentence_id":0,"text":"The Daily News, originally the Palo Alto Daily News, is a free newspaper owned by MediaNews Group and located in Menlo Park.","title":"The Daily News (Palo Alto)"},{"sentence_id":1,"text":" It was formerly published seven days a week and at one point had a circulation of 67,000 (a figure that included five zoned editions which no longer exist).","title":"The Daily News (Palo Alto)"},{"sentence_id":2,"text":" The "Daily News" is distributed in red newspaper racks and in stores, coffee shops, restaurants, schools and major workplaces.","title":"The Daily News (Palo Alto)"},{"sentence_id":3,"text":" As of April 7, 2009 the paper ceased to be published as "The Palo Alto Daily News" and was consolidated with other San Francisco Peninsula "Daily News" titles; it published five days a week, Tuesday through Saturday.","title":"The Daily News (Palo Alto)"},{"sentence_id":4,"text":" Weekday editions were delivered to selected homes.","title":"The Daily News (Palo Alto)"},{"sentence_id":5,"text":" While continuing to publish daily online, "The Daily News" cut its print edition back to three days a week in 2013, and one day a week in 2015.","title":"The Daily News (Palo Alto)"}],"peer_messages":["Unverified peer answer: Because the Bangor Daily News ran a poll of policymakers that ranked him the ninth most influential person in Maine politics.
